WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.380
<v 0>The notebook allows the user to simulate the metabolic response to a stimulation</v>

2
00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.450
Step pulse first,

3
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.160
some useful packages are read in the initial concentrations and the

4
00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.440
parameters are read from JSON files.

5
00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:20.280
If you want to modify an initial value or parameter,

6
00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:23.160
you can do it in the code after reading the JSON file.

7
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.910
In the next section, you can configure the stimulus and age properties.

8
00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.900
You can specify the stimulus, onset and duration here,

9
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.660
as well as the total time of the simulation.

10
00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:43.830
The most important parameter is the age, which can be set in a pull-down menu.

11
00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:50.850
Next, we choose how the sodium potassium A TP ACE is handled with CAT being the

12
00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.200
default and full,

13
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.830
allowing consideration of the effect of ageing on catalytic subunits.

14
00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:02.580
The code also contains a switch for default or custom configurations,

15
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:03.750
which can be set here.

16
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.100
If the user chooses a custom configuration,

17
00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.550
the use of specific custom routines can be indicated.

18
00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:19.110
The type of stimulus is set here. You can choose synaptic stimulation,

19
00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:22.110
or both synaptic and norepinephrine stimulation,

20
00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.420
or a current injection.

21
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:28.760
The user can also opt to save the output. The

22
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:36.390
next section contains the logic needed to adjust the model for the young or aged

23
00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:37.223
states.

24
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.840
<v 1>One can also modify the model to support a custom configuration.</v>

25
00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.010
The stimulus effects are imposed in.

26
00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:57.720
<v 0>The next code block. In this section,</v>

27
00:01:57.870 --> 00:01:59.940
the equations are set up. There are.

28
00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:01.590
<v 1>A large number of equations</v>

29
00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:23.520
functions to support the differential. Equation solving are defined here.

30
00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:28.290
Then the system can be solved

31
00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:37.680
if the user has chosen to save the output. It is then saved.

32
00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:44.160
The variable indices.

33
00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.500
<v 0>In their identities are listed for convenience.</v>

34
00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:54.960
The user can select which variables to plot by checking or unchecking the check

35
00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.560
boxes. Two different selection sets are included in the default selections.

36
00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:05.230
<v 1>Finally, the results are plotted.</v>

